  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Working Through Colonial Collections shows early scholarly traction and notable wider use. The book has been cited once, downloaded 3,338 times through OAPEN, and it is already appearing in teaching-related discussion online. Its record also includes two Crossref attention mentions and funding support from KU Leuven, Humboldt-Universit\u00e4t zu Berlin, and CARMAH, suggesting sustained institutional and public interest around its themes of colonialism, ethnography, and museum practice.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Margareta von Oswald\u2019s Working Through Colonial Collections is a 2022 scholarly book from Leuven University Press that examines how ethnological museums confront colonial legacies, with a specific focus on the Africa department of the Ethnological Museum in Berlin and the contested move to the Humboldt Forum. The impact record points to a work that is being taken up both academically and in broader conversations about heritage and museums, although the citation base is still small and should be read as an early signal rather than mature scholarly consensus.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest quantitative sign of reach is usage: the book has been downloaded 3,338 times via OAPEN. That pattern indicates active readership and suggests the book is circulating beyond a narrow specialist audience. Its monthly download record shows recurring attention across the year rather than a one-off spike, which is consistent with sustained use of the open-access version.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is present but modest so far, with one citation recorded in 2024. That citing article, published in Girasol/Revista humanidades, is situated in the intersecting areas of cultural heritage management, archaeology and cultural heritage, and indigenous history, and it comes from Argentina. Taken together, this suggests that the book is already informing related debates beyond German museum studies, especially where colonial collections, heritage circulation, and museum interpretation overlap.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There are also signs of broader public and teaching-related engagement. The record includes two Crossref attention mentions, a YouTube discussion that references the book, and a WorldCat holdings trace, all of which point to discoverability and circulation in library and informal learning contexts. OpenAIRE also records the book as available in open form, which likely supports this reach. Overall, the evidence shows a book with clear readership and emerging citation impact, particularly in heritage-focused scholarship.</p>",
